  def svs-ng-at(state, n, nmin, k, at) = at >= 0 && at < len(state[0]) ? take(k, drop(at, state[0])) : svs-ng-try(state, n - 1, nmin, k);
  // a sequence that ends gives its blocks back (the last owner of a block frees it; a prefix-cache entry that holds one keeps it)
  def svs-give-back(env, s) {
   let r = svb-release(svs-pool(env), s[24]);
   1;
  }
  // ── shedding: a sequence whose client is gone, in ANY phase ──
  // A client that is gone will never read its reply. A sequence it left QUEUED, PREFILLING or DECODING is ended by name ("owner-gone") and gives its blocks back
  // at once, so a dead client's 100000-id prompt does not hold the one prefill lane for minutes and its blocks do not sit in the pool. gone is a function of the sequence's id.
  def svs-gone-call(gone, id) { let f = gone; f(id); }
  def svs-gone?(gone, s) = svs-gone-call(gone, s[0]);
  def svs-shed(S, env, gone) {
